# 1 Introduction {#ch:intro}

Basilisk (<http://basilisk.fr>) is a free library for solving partial
differential equations on adaptive Cartesian meshes, written by Stéphane
Popinet as the successor of Gerris \[[9](references)\]. It is used mostly for
fluid mechanics: incompressible and variable-density flows, interfaces with
surface tension, shallow-water and multilayer ocean models, and flows around
solid bodies.

A Basilisk simulation is one C file. It includes a grid, a solver and a few
modules, then defines *events*: functions that run at given iterations or
times. The translator `qcc` turns the Basilisk extensions to C
(`foreach()`, `event`, field declarations) into plain C and calls
the C compiler. Nothing in the numerics is hidden in a binary. Every equation
in this guide is a loop in one of the header files under `$BASILISK`,
and the guide quotes those loops.

## Who this guide is for

This guide is for someone who knows some fluid mechanics and C and wants to
use Basilisk with an understanding of what it computes. It has three parts.

Part I is practical. It covers the Basilisk language, how to set
up, compile, run and restart a case, how to get data out, four complete
worked examples that were run for this guide, and the mistakes that most
often break a run. Read it first and run the examples.

Part II explains the numerical method. It starts from general
CFD ideas (finite volumes, stability, upwinding, the CFL condition,
implicit time stepping, projection methods), then follows one time step of
the incompressible Navier--Stokes solver: the grid, advection,
momentum-conserving transport for two fluids, viscosity, body and
interfacial forces, the pressure projection, the multigrid solver, the
volume-of-fluid method, boundary conditions and embedded solids. Each
derivation is followed by the source line that computes it.

Part III describes the other solvers and modules in the library
and when to use them.

## The pieces of a typical simulation

Figure 1.1 shows the files a two-phase simulation with an embedded
solid includes, and what each one contributes. The Navier--Stokes solver
(`navier-stokes/centered.h`) owns the time loop and calls a fixed
sequence of events. The other modules plug into that sequence by
*overloading* an event: `reduced.h` adds gravity inside the
`acceleration` event, `two-phase.h` sets density and viscosity in
the `properties` event, and so on.

