## How to read the boxes

Two kinds of boxes appear in the text. An *In the code* box quotes
the source line that computes the equation just derived. A titled concept
box explains a general CFD idea or a rule to remember.

> **Notation used everywhere**
>
> $\Delta$ is the size of the current cell (`Delta` in the code), $\Delta t$ the time
> step (`dt`), and superscripts $n$, $n+\tfrac{1}{2}$, $n+1$ are time levels.
> Cell centres have integer indices $i, j, k$; faces have half indices, so
> $u_{f,{i+\frac 12}}$ is the face velocity between cells $i$ and $i+1$. In Basilisk
> code `s[]` is the value in the current cell, `s[1]` its right
> neighbour and `s[-1]` its left neighbour; `s[0,1]` is the neighbour
> above. A face field value `uf.x[]` lives on the *left* face of the
> current cell, so `uf.x[]` is $u_{f,{i-\frac 12}}$ and `uf.x[1]` is
> $u_{f,{i+\frac 12}}$. A full symbol table is in [chapter A](A_notation).

## Where to find more

The source files are the reference documentation: each header starts with
a commented explanation, and the same text is rendered on
<http://basilisk.fr/src/>. The directories `$BASILISK/test` and
`$BASILISK/examples` contain several hundred complete programs, most
of them with plots and comparisons against reference solutions. When you
need a feature, the fastest route is usually to find a test that uses it.

